    <section class="msv-section">
      <div class="msv-section-label">FAQs</div>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Phone and Utilities in Spain</h2>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">Do I need a NIE to set up utilities in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">For electricity and gas contracts with the main providers, yes — you will typically need a NIE or Spanish identity document. However, some providers will accept a passport and proof of address for initial setup, particularly on short-term or temporary contracts. Mobile SIMs, especially prepaid (prepago), can usually be purchased with just a passport. Getting your NIE as soon as you arrive is strongly recommended to avoid complications.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">Which mobile network has the best coverage in rural Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Movistar (Telefonica) has the best and most extensive coverage in rural and remote areas of Spain, including mountain areas and inland villages. Vodafone and Orange have strong coverage in most populated areas but can have gaps in remote zones. Digi and Lebara use other networks' infrastructure (via roaming agreements) and may have notable gaps in rural areas. If you live outside a city or major town, Movistar is the safest choice, despite being more expensive than the alternatives.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What is the cheapest internet provider in Spain in 2026?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Digi is consistently the cheapest fibre broadband provider in Spain where their own network is available, offering 1Gbps symmetrical fibre for around 20–25 euros per month in 2026. Where Digi is not available, Orange and Movistar's entry-level fibre plans are competitive. Prices across all providers have dropped significantly as Spain's fibre coverage has expanded. A combined fibre plus mobile bundle from any of the big three (Movistar, Vodafone, Orange) often works out cheaper than separate contracts.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">Can I set up electricity before I have my NIE?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">In practice, many rental contracts include utilities or the contract remains in the landlord's name, making this less of an issue. If you need to set up a new electricity contract yourself without a NIE, some providers may accept a passport number as a temporary measure. However, this is not guaranteed and varies by provider. The cleanest solution is to ask your landlord to help with the initial setup, then transfer the contract to your name once you have your NIE.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">How long does it take to get internet installed in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">For fibre broadband in a building that already has fibre infrastructure (the majority of urban buildings), installation appointments with major providers are typically scheduled within 5–10 working days. Digi may take slightly longer in some areas. If your building needs new fibre run to it (less common in cities), the wait can extend to several weeks. Delays are more common in rural areas and older buildings. If you need internet immediately on arrival, a mobile data SIM with a generous data allowance is a reliable bridge.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What is the CUPS code and why do I need it?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">CUPS (Codigo Unico de Punto de Suministro) is the unique reference number for your electricity or gas supply point — think of it as the address for your meter rather than for your flat. You need it when setting up a new electricity or gas contract, or when switching supplier, as it tells the new provider exactly which meter they are taking over. You can find your CUPS on any existing electricity or gas bill for the property, or ask your landlord. Without it, the provider cannot process your contract.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">Can I keep my UK phone number in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">You cannot easily keep a UK mobile number as your primary number in Spain. Since Brexit, UK numbers used in EU countries are subject to operator roaming policies that have changed significantly — many UK operators now charge extra for EU roaming or exclude it entirely from budget plans. The practical approach used by most British expats is to get a Spanish SIM for daily use in Spain, while keeping a UK SIM active on a minimal plan for receiving UK calls and maintaining UK contacts. VOIP services like Skype Numbers or Google Voice can provide a UK number at low cost.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">How does Spanish electricity billing work?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Spanish electricity bills have two main components. The termino de potencia (power term) is a fixed monthly charge based on your contracted power capacity in kilowatts — you pay this regardless of how much electricity you use, so it pays to review whether you are contracted for the right capacity. The termino de consumo (consumption term) is the variable charge for actual kWh used. Both components attract IVA (VAT at 21%) and a small electricity tax. Bills are usually monthly and paid by direct debit.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What if my landlord pays the utilities?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Many rental contracts in Spain, particularly for furnished flats or short-term lets, include utilities within the monthly rent. This is convenient and avoids the need to set up your own contracts, but it means you have no control over the provider, tariff, or service quality. If utilities are included, your rental contract should specify what is included (electricity, gas, water, internet), any fair-use consumption limits, and who is responsible if there is a fault. For long-term rentals, it is more common for the tenant to take over or set up their own contracts.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">How easy is it to switch electricity or internet provider in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Switching electricity supplier in Spain's liberalised market is straightforward and supply is never interrupted. Contact the new supplier with your CUPS code and NIE, and they manage the entire process — your lights stay on throughout. For broadband, switching typically requires a new installation appointment and a notice period of 15–30 days with your existing provider. Check your current contract's minimum term before switching — early termination fees apply on most Spanish broadband and bundle contracts, typically 50–100% of remaining monthly payments for the contracted period.</div>
      </details>
    </section>

